# Phase Commits

Work is committed one phase at a time. A phase is committed only once it is genuinely
finished, so the branch history reads as a sequence of complete, working steps.

Nothing is staged, committed, or pushed without the user's approval in the chat.

## When a phase is ready

A phase is ready for a commit proposal when both of these are true:

- The tests for that phase pass. Run `npm test` and confirm it is green.
- The technical PRD in `ai-workspace/` has been updated with the phase status and details.

Until both hold, the phase is not finished. Do not propose a commit for partial work in the
middle of a phase, and never commit while its tests are failing.

## Propose, then wait

When the phase's work is done and the PRD is updated, stop and show the exact commands you
would run, in order, together with the commit message:

```bash
git add migrations/0002_create_mcq_tables.sql migrations/mcq-schema.test.ts ai-workspace/mcq-crud-technical-prd.md
git commit -m "phase 1: add MCQ tables migration and schema tests"
git push
```

Then wait. Do not run `git add`, `git commit`, or `git push` until the user says go in the
chat. Approving one phase's commit is not approval of the next one.

Once the user approves, run the commands as shown. Push after the user approves, in the same
approved batch as the commit — never ahead of the approval, and not as a separate step
decided on your own afterwards.

## What the commit looks like

- Stage only the files belonging to that phase. Use explicit paths with `git add <path>`
  rather than `git add -A` or `git add .`, which pick up unrelated work.
- Commit with the phase number and a short description of what the phase delivered, for
  example `phase 3: add MCQ list and delete endpoints`.
- One phase per commit. Do not combine two phases into a single commit, and do not split
  one phase across several commits.

## What belongs in the commit

- PRD updates and D1 migrations that describe or support the phase go in the **same**
  commit as the code they relate to. Do not commit them separately.
- Anything not part of the phase stays out, including unrelated formatting or refactors.

## Fixes after a phase is closed

Once a phase is committed, that commit is closed. A later correction gets its own commit
prefixed `fix:`, with the related phase named in the commit body:

```bash
git commit -m "fix: reject blank MCQ option text" -m "Follow-up to phase 3."
```

A fix commit is proposed and approved the same way: show the commands, then wait for go.

Do not amend or rewrite a phase commit that has already been pushed.
